    "case_for": "Pulsars opened a new observational window on compact stars and have enabled enduring studies of gravitation and dense matter. Bell Burnell identified the first pulsar in the data, giving this discovery a clear central attribution, and its importance has been established over decades.",
    "case_against": "The discovery arose within a larger observing program, so a single laureate cannot represent every contribution. The relationship to earlier recognition for radio astronomy also needs careful historical review to establish a distinct awardable contribution.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Bell Burnell identified the unusual signal, but the boundary between this discovery and the radio astronomy work recognized in an earlier prize needs review.",
        "response": "I agree that this boundary must be established before settling the citation. The pulsar discovery has a specific observational claim and lasting significance; the committee should assess whether that claim is sufficiently distinct from the earlier recognition."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "pearce-mark",
        "point": "Recognizing Bell Burnell would address a consequential historical omission, though a prize for the discovery alone would leave out the wider team and later researchers.",
        "response": "The historical omission is relevant, but it cannot substitute for the scientific case. Bell Burnell's direct role in the first detection supports individual recognition, while the citation should stay focused on that discovery rather than imply she alone built the broader field."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "How to distinguish the specific pulsar discovery from radio astronomy work recognized earlier.",
      "Whether historical evidence supports Bell Burnell as the central individual contributor within the larger observing program."
    ],
